  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Sarcoscypha occidentalis, commonly known as the stalked scarlet cup, is a striking fungus characterized by its brilliant scarlet-red cup-shaped fruiting bodies. This wood-decay fungus belongs to the Elf Cups and Allies family (Sarcoscyphaceae) and is found in temperate forests throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Bright Scarlet Cup",
      "description": "Distinctive vivid red, cup-shaped fruiting body with a smooth inner surface and paler, hairy outer surface",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Distinct Stalk",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies are elevated on a prominent, thick stalk or stem that is whitish or pale colored",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and Shape",
      "description": "Cups typically 1-2 inches wide and appear in early spring before many other fungi",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Stalked scarlet cups fruit on decaying hardwood logs and branches, particularly in moist, shaded woodlands throughout New England during late winter and early spring.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The brilliant scarlet color of this fungus is thought to attract insects that help disperse its spores, making it one of nature's most visually striking fungal fruiting bodies.",
